[Music]  in seventh grade I looked through a  microscope for the first time and I saw  a Paramecium swimming around and I was  just fascinated I never wanted to move  from a microscope again that was where I  wanted to spend my life I'm Jo  Handelsman I'm a microbiologist it was  just like that look huh how does that  fit together and it was clearly girl  science girls can't do that when you ask  kids to draw scientists most will draw  the same image white bald guy with a  pointy beard little glasses and a white  lab coat and then when I was in 10th  grade I had a biology teacher mr. post  Leone and he convinced me that I could  do science as a girl and finally when I  got to graduate school I started seeing  role models in science women who had  done what I wanted to do they'd become  biologists professors they did research  and realized those two things can go  together you can be female and you can  be a science professor you don't have to  look like those guys  [Music]  microbiome is a community of  microorganisms associated with some  environment from the ocean to the soil  to the human gut and it's rapidly  evolving science we are beginning to  understand the roles that they play in  regulating our own health and our immune  systems much of chronic disease is now  being associated with changes in our  microbiomes obesity heart disease asthma  the list is very extensive if we can  control those microbial communities we  can take enormous advantage of them I  was one of the science advisors for  President Obama during the last three  years of his administration welcome to  the White House Science Fair he is a  wonderful fan of science this stuff's  really cool it was the high point of his  day having a discussion about  initiatives to change American science  precision medicine is a science trying  to figure out what's different between  individual people that makes one person  respond to a treatment and another  person not  President Obama was personally committed  to it tonight I'm launching a new  precision medicine initiative he was  very excited about making this front and  center of the future of American  medicine the right treatment at the  right time a bill was passed that had  about four point eight billion dollars  would you do this I think it's one of  our most successful initiatives from my  time at the White House  I was in the habit of giving President  Obama stuff microbes and these were  little miniature microorganisms that  represented in real microbes the very  first one I gave him was of a  streptococcus flesh-eating bacteria one  of the scientists was just totally  baffled what are you gonna do with it  little coy it was quite an experience to  work with him at one of the world's  problems was that antibiotics that we  have no longer worked with many of our  diseases but industry has kind of lost  interest we're trying to rebuild that  interest soil bacteria are these  prolific producers of antibiotic  activity about 75% of the antibiotics in  current use are from soil bacteria  and there's some pretty good evidence  that bacteria are producing antibiotics  for sending messages to each other and  so we are just co-opting something that  the bacteria are making to use as drugs  for our own purposes  small world is something that I started  in 2012 to teach science through solving  the worlds of problems with young bright  scientists in the classroom they would  learn about science through culturing  bacteria from soil for production of  antibiotics it's been a really  extraordinary process to see all of the  students and their instructors  passionate about antibiotic discovery  from soil  [Music]  above all failure is okay there are  messages that are sent in science about  failure that just shouldn't be sent  failure is a part of science it doesn't  mean you're not good at it it doesn't  mean to stop it just means to go back  and do it again I so often hear students  say oh I'm not a science person I always  thought I was just sort of unfit for  science and I never knew why and I'm  sure part of it was just not seeing  people who looked like me in science  we all bring these stereotypes and  prejudices to our decisions about people  and it also means that we can all undo  that  the future of science is so much  brighter and stronger because there are  more women and minorities and people  with lots of different backgrounds but  it could be even stronger  [Music]

